What is a Linked List?

A Linked List is a linear data structure where each element (node) stores:

Data
Address (Reference) of the next node

Unlike an array, the elements are not stored next to each other in memory.
Example

Suppose we want to store:

10 20 30 40
Array
Index : 0   1   2   3
Value :10  20  30  40

All values are stored continuously in memory.
Why do we use a Linked List?

Imagine you have an array.

10 20 30 40

Now you want to insert 15.

Array becomes

10 15 20 30 40

To do this, you have to move 20, 30, and 40 one position to the right.

This shifting takes O(n) time.

In a Linked List:

10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40

Insert 15:

10 -> 15 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40

You only change a few pointers (references). No need to shift all elements.

That's why insertion is efficient.

